<HelpLayout title="No or minimal equipment" description="Log bodyweight training in Badger: push-ups to pull-ups, timed holds, and when to move to a harder version.">
  <h1>Training with no or minimal equipment</h1>
  <p>You train with your body weight, maybe a pull-up bar, a dip belt or an assisted machine. Badger logs reps and holds as easily as barbell sets, and it tells you when a move has become easy enough to swap for a harder one.</p>

  <h2>Your first 5 minutes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <strong>Today</strong> tab and tap <strong>New workout</strong>.</li>
    <li>Search "push-up" and pick it. Push-ups, pull-ups, chin-ups and dips log reps only, so there is no weight to fill in.</li>
    <li>Enter your reps and tap <strong>Save set</strong>. The first time, Badger asks how you want to log. Pick <strong>Log as you go</strong> and each Save set logs the set as done.</li>
    <li>Add a plank. Tap <strong>Start timer</strong> under the Duration field, pick <strong>Stopwatch</strong> and tap <strong>Start</strong>. Tap <strong>Stop</strong> when you drop, then <strong>Save set</strong>.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>End workout</strong> when you are done.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Move up a version</h2>
  <p>When the same reps come easily, a line on the training screen tells you so. It appears once every working set reaches its target for 3 sessions running.</p>
  <p>The target is the reps or hold time your routine plans, or 8 reps (30 seconds on a hold) when there is no plan. For example: <strong>8 reps on every set, 3 sessions running. Ready for a harder version.</strong></p>
  <p>Under it, <strong>Swap to Decline Push-Up</strong> changes the exercise for today only. Where a weighted version exists, a second link offers that too. Each move has a short ladder, easiest first:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Push: Knee Push-Up, Incline Push-Up, Push-Up, Decline Push-Up, Diamond Push-Up, Archer Push-Up, Weighted Push-Up</li>
    <li>Pull: Assisted Pull-Up, Negative Pull-Up, Pull-Up, Weighted Pull-Up</li>
    <li>Squat: Bodyweight Squat, Split Squat, Bulgarian Split Squat</li>
    <li>Front hold: Plank, Hollow Body Hold</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Dips, rows, pistols, glute bridges, leg raises and side planks have ladders too. All of them are listed under <a href="/help/progression/#bodyweight-ladders">The harder versions</a>. To change the 3, set <strong>Progressive overload trigger</strong> in <strong>Settings &rarr; Coaching</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Holds with the stopwatch</h2>
  <p>A plank, a dead hang or a hollow body hold logs time only. Tap <strong>Start timer</strong> under the Duration field, pick <strong>Stopwatch</strong> and tap <strong>Start</strong>. It counts you in with "3, 2, 1, Go", then calls out each whole minute you have held. <strong>Stop</strong> fills in your time, and you tap <strong>Save set</strong>.</p>
  <p>With a target time in the Duration field, the timer opens as a countdown to that time instead. You can switch either way. See <a href="/help/logging/#stopwatch">Stopwatch or countdown</a>.</p>

  <h2>Weighted and assisted versions</h2>
  <p>Once you add a belt or a vest, log on the weighted version: Weighted Pull-Up, Weighted Chin-Up, Weighted Dip or Weighted Push-Up. Enter only the weight you add. A set reads <em>+25 lbs &times; 6</em>, or <em>BW &times; 8</em> with nothing added, and records count the added weight.</p>
  <p>On an assisted machine, log Assisted Pull-Up or Assisted Dip and enter the assistance the machine gives. Less assistance is the harder set, so a lower number counts as your record. See <a href="/help/exercises/#bodyweight">Bodyweight, weighted and assisted exercises</a>.</p>

  <h2>Ignore the rest for now</h2>
  <p>Plate calculators, bars, gyms and progression rules are for loaded training. You can leave them alone.</p>

  <h2>When you are ready</h2>
  <p>To train the same moves on set days, <a href="/guides/program-runner/">the program guide</a> shows how to save them as a routine. To see your records and trends, <a href="/guides/optimizer/">the data guide</a> shows where they live.</p>

  <p>Want the full picture? See <a href="/guides/overview/">everything Badger does</a>.</p>
</HelpLayout>
